historically communities addressed  shortages by augmenting supplies usually  by building large infrastructure  projects such as dams pipelines and  aqueducts and more recently desalination  plants but this focus on source  augmentation has depleted source waters  around the world and undermined global  freshwater ecosystems let me give you a  couple of examples in a typical year  many of the most important rivers in the  world such as the Nile in Egypt the  Yellow River in China and the Colorado  River in the u.s. no longer reach their  deltas in California transfer water to  Los Angeles has desiccated Owens Lake  which now generates roughly six percent  of the asthma and lung cancer inducing  airborne dust in the u.s. in this paper  we're describing an alternative vision  in which the focus shifts from supply  augmentation to improving what we call  water productivity which basically means  getting more out of less water  one of the most obvious examples is loss  of water from water distribution  networks in developing countries for  example approximately fifty percent of  the water that goes into the pipeline  systems from the water treatment plants  is lost either through leakage in the  pipelines or poor accounting imagine  trying to run a business where you lost  fifty percent of your product I mean it  wouldn't be very viable even in well-run  utilities for example in the United  States or in Europe the loss rates are  on the order of ten percent so there's  there's definitely improvement that we  can make in that regard another example  of how we we waste water is is run off  rainfall runs off of our roofs in our  streets and directly into local streams  and the pollutant loading and flow  velocities that are associated with that  runoff are really a deadly one-two punch  to our freshwater ecosystems  in countries you know the number one  concern really has to do with basic  human needs safe drinking water and  sanitation those are the priorities in  developed countries typically we have  access to safe drinking water and we  have adequate sanitation and the problem  is more typically a biodiversity issue  in other words the ecosystem is  suffering because of lack of freshwater  and both of those challenges can be  addressed by improving water  productivity although the details of how  that's done are going to be very  community specific  the first is substitution on the second  one would be regeneration and the third  is reduction substitution involves using  lower quality water in place of higher  quality water where appropriate treated  wastewater might not be suitable for  drinking but it can be perfectly  suitable for landscape irrigation using  treated waste water fluoridation saves  drinking water ms improves drinking  water productivity another example is or  a second approach that's discussed in  the paper is what we call regeneration  which involves treating lower quality  water so that it is for all practical  purposes just as good as high quality  water an example is potable reuse in  which wastewater is treated using  conventional and advanced techniques and  then added back to the water supply in  Windhoek Namibia for example highly  treated wastewater has been added to the  potable water distribution system more  or less continuously since the late  1960s with no obvious adverse health  effects among the population of several  hundred thousand people the third  approach that we talked about in the  paper is called reduction which involves  basically using less water to accomplish  the same task a great example is dual  flush toilets by installing dual flush  toilets which flush different volumes of  water for urine and feces a city the  size of a million people can reduce  municipal water use by upwards of thirty  percent and substantially reduce  electricity costs  comes how do we how do we move from a  situation of near crisis to a more  sustainable situation and one of the  ways that we can begin to address that  is through changing the relationship  with water which involves rethinking not  not trying to get more water which has  kind of been the historical focus but  but really instead we thinking about how  we use the water and how we can use the  water more productively  you
 

